A realistic strict policy for an SPA with a JSON API and one analytics vendor:

```http
Content-Security-Policy:
  default-src 'self';
  script-src 'nonce-rAnd0m2024' 'strict-dynamic' https: 'unsafe-inline';
  style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';
  img-src 'self' data: https://cdn.example.com;
  connect-src 'self' https://api.example.com https://analytics.vendor.com;
  font-src 'self';
  object-src 'none';
  base-uri 'self';
  form-action 'self';
  frame-ancestors 'none';
  report-uri /csp-report
```

Two lines look wrong and aren't:

- **`'unsafe-inline'` next to a nonce.** Browsers that understand nonces *ignore* `'unsafe-inline'` when one is present, so it's a fallback for ancient browsers only. Same trick with `https:` — it's ignored wherever `'strict-dynamic'` is supported.
- **`'unsafe-inline'` in `style-src`** is a deliberate concession. Inline style is far less dangerous than inline script, and every CSS-in-JS library needs it. Locking styles down is a later, optional step.

The nonce must be **regenerated per response** from a cryptographic RNG. A build-time constant is the same as no nonce at all.
